[38;5;231m⏺[39m Last question before I lay out approaches. The fork: how a "shared across the app" session module gets loaded, given
  [38;5;153mapp.js[39m is a classic script and [38;5;153msrc/*.js[39m are CommonJS for Node — and nothing bridges them.

  - [1mClassic script + one global namespace[0m — [38;5;153msession.js[39m loaded by a [38;5;153m<script>[39m tag before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m, exposing a single
    [38;5;153mAppSession[39m object. Matches the pattern already in [38;5;153mindex.html[39m, needs no tooling, and every future form gets it by
    adding one script tag. Costs: a global, and no sharing with the Node-side CommonJS files (which don't need it — the
    tracking is browser-only).
  - [1mConvert the browser side to ES modules[0m — [38;5;153mindex.html[39m becomes [38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m, [38;5;153msession.js[39m uses [38;5;153mexport[39m/[38;5;153mimport[39m.
    No globals, scales cleanly to many forms, no bundler required. Costs: every page's script tag changes, and the app
    can no longer be opened via [38;5;153mfile://[39m — ES modules require serving over http, which this repo has no server for.
  - [1mDual-format module[0m — works under both CommonJS and the browser global. Buys Node-side reuse you don't currently
    need; costs boilerplate on every file. This is the YAGNI casualty.

  Both real options keep the same key property: all reads and writes go through one session module, so the storage
  choice stays swappable. My recommendation is the classic-script namespace — it's the existing local pattern, and
  converting the page-loading model is more restructuring than tracking a userId warrants. ESM stays available later as
  a mechanical migration.
